  RealizedBehavior, RealizedRules, UnrealizedBehavior,
  approach_triggers,
}

data roadmap_interaction_note: String = "The roadmap dashboard's interaction rows (slice 2 of roadmap-onto-register, manager-signed): the dispatch button declares Approach and Dispatch, and its stylesheet responses are DERIVED from these rows through roadmap_realize_behavior — the register law (no dead hovers; responses derived) applied to the second site. Approach realizes as the border brighten over the shared approach_triggers (hover + focus-visible, one authority with the site pages); Dispatch realizes as the OnActive press receipt — background flips to the border material for the pressed frame, the CSS-layer receipt; the belt-side dispatch receipt is the served effect (belt_dispatch_node, serving layer) and never this module's concern. The realize fold mirrors moodboard's shape (RealizedRules | UnrealizedBehavior, unrealized countable); the Phase-A lift unifies the two folds behind a realize-parametric L2 carrier — declared convergence, not a silent fork. Known residue, named: the roadmap's a:hover underline is a hand StaticRule with no declared element (links are unmodeled site-wide today); it joins the element roster when the site models link elements, and the stray-response census belongs to the lift."

data roadmap_physical_behavior_note: String = "The dispatch button's full mechanical story, as rows. Before this it had two: the edge brightens on approach, the background flips on press — both responses of APPEARANCE, which is exactly the critique the analog root principle answers, because a control that only changes colour is a diagram that lights up rather than an object that moves. The four added rows are the travel.

Read as one mechanism at successive positions: Approach lifts it 1 px toward the hand while the edge brightens (two rows, one verb — genuinely two answers to one event). Dispatch is the engagement, and it fires on pointer-DOWN through OnActive rather than on release, because acknowledgment belongs to the press; the control travels down and compresses. Await holds it compressed while the request is in flight, deeper than the press, because a request in flight IS the control still actuated — and because a spring needs travel to return over. Accept releases it into unity on the settle curve, which overshoots and comes back: the detent catching.

Await and Accept realize as StateRule rather than ResponseRule for a structural reason, not a stylistic one: their trigger is a server answer arriving long after the pointer is gone, and CSS has no pseudo-class for that. So they key on the SAME class names the client program flips (dispatch_class_requested / dispatch_class_ok), which is what keeps the CSS and the JS on one authority instead of two hand-matched string sets.

The sound toggle joins the roster because it is a control, and the register's coverage law does not exempt small ones — an unresponsive mute button would be precisely the dead hover the law exists to forbid. It gets travel and brightening and no sound of its own: a mute control that clicked when you muted it would be arguing with you."

data roadmap_elements: List<InteractiveElement> = [
  InteractiveElement { element: DispatchBtnElement, verbs: [Approach, Dispatch, Await, Accept] },
  InteractiveElement { element: SoundToggleElement, verbs: [Approach, Dispatch] },
]

data roadmap_timing_assignment_note: String = "Which clock each verb runs on, reusing the existing scale rather than minting near-misses (operator, 2026-07-24). APPROACH runs on reveal_deliberate (160 ms): an approach is a reveal — the control coming toward the hand — and the earlier 90 ms on the brighten row was the press clock borrowed for a gesture that is not a press. DISPATCH runs on respond_fast (90 ms), the press-acknowledge clock, because engagement must land under the finger and anything slower reads as lag. AWAIT also runs on respond_fast: the control latching down when the request leaves is an engagement, not a reveal. ACCEPT runs on settle_spring, which is restore_gentle's 240 ms carrying the overshoot curve — the only mint, and only on the easing axis."

data roadmap_behaviors: List<BehaviorRow> = [
  BehaviorRow { element: DispatchBtnElement, verb: Approach, response: Brighten, timing: reveal_deliberate },
  BehaviorRow { element: DispatchBtnElement, verb: Approach, response: Lift, timing: reveal_deliberate },
  BehaviorRow { element: DispatchBtnElement, verb: Dispatch, response: Receipt, timing: respond_fast },
  BehaviorRow { element: DispatchBtnElement, verb: Dispatch, response: Depress, timing: respond_fast },
  BehaviorRow { element: DispatchBtnElement, verb: Await, response: Depress, timing: respond_fast },
  BehaviorRow { element: DispatchBtnElement, verb: Accept, response: Settle, timing: settle_spring },
  BehaviorRow { element: SoundToggleElement, verb: Approach, response: Brighten, timing: reveal_deliberate },
  BehaviorRow { element: SoundToggleElement, verb: Approach, response: Lift, timing: reveal_deliberate },
  BehaviorRow { element: SoundToggleElement, verb: Dispatch, response: Depress, timing: respond_fast },
]

data roadmap_detent_note: String = "The dispatch button's detent table — the mechanical spec the CSS is DERIVED from, pinned as data because a transition that connects two equal transforms is inert (gunbc.design.interaction.detent_table_note).

The positions read as one latching mechanism. REST sits at unity with no travel. HOVER rises 1 px toward the hand at unity scale — a reveal, not a compression. PRESSED is the finger's own travel: 1 px down and 3% in. REQUESTED is the control LATCHED, and it sits DEEPER than the finger left it (4% in, no vertical travel) which is the one choice here worth defending. A momentary switch would return to rest when released; this control does not, because the request is still in flight and the honest analog is a latching push-button that holds its engaged position until the mechanism releases it. The depth is also what buys the settle its travel: this curve's peak excursion is about a tenth of the delta, so the doc's illustrative 0.985 would have produced a spring under a tenth of a pixel on a chip-sized control — a beat that has to be believed rather than seen. ACCEPTED returns to unity, so the visible event is a 4% growth carrying a small overshoot, and the terminal position is the resting one exactly as the contract says terminals are 1.0.

ACCEPTED and REST share a transform deliberately: a settled control IS at rest, and the accepted→rest edge is not one that needs to beat. Which edges DO need to beat is declared separately in roadmap_detent_edges, so the sharing is a stated fact rather than an accident the distinctness check has to be weakened to tolerate."

data detent_rest: String = "rest"
data detent_hover: String = "hover"
data detent_pressed: String = "pressed"
data detent_requested: String = "requested"
data detent_accepted: String = "accepted"

data roadmap_dispatch_detents: List<Detent> = concat(
  house_pointer_detents(rest_state: detent_rest, hover_state: detent_hover, pressed_state: detent_pressed),
  [
    Detent {
      state: detent_requested,
      reach: ByTrigger { trigger: OnStateClass { class_name: dispatch_class_requested } },
      travel: NoTravel,
      scale_per_mille: hold_scale_per_mille,
    },
    Detent {
      state: detent_accepted,
      reach: ByTrigger { trigger: OnStateClass { class_name: dispatch_class_ok } },
      travel: NoTravel,
      scale_per_mille: rest_scale_per_mille,
    },
  ])

data roadmap_sound_toggle_detents: List<Detent> = house_pointer_detents(
  rest_state: detent_rest,
  hover_state: detent_hover,
  pressed_state: detent_pressed,
)

data roadmap_detent_edges: List<DetentEdge> = [
  DetentEdge { from_state: detent_rest, to_state: detent_hover },
  DetentEdge { from_state: detent_hover, to_state: detent_pressed },
  DetentEdge { from_state: detent_rest, to_state: detent_pressed },
  DetentEdge { from_state: detent_pressed, to_state: detent_requested },
  DetentEdge { from_state: detent_requested, to_state: detent_accepted },
]

data dispatch_brighten_neutral_note: String = "Approach emphasis on the FILLED instrument is band-neutral (operator ruling 2026-07-24): the earlier target was var(--focus) — brass — and brass is hue-ambiguous next to the band fills (on mint it reads venom-adjacent brown, on venom it reads mint-adjacent yellow-green), so hover appeared to INVERT the state colors. The theme's ink (var(--text)) is the one color that reads as pure emphasis on every fill under both themes, so approach = ink ring + the existing lift, and never a hue that could be mistaken for a band. Brass remains the focus material elsewhere (the review mark); this retarget is the instrument's own row."

fn dispatch_btn_brighten_rules(timing: TimingToken) -> List<BuildRule> {
  map(approach_triggers(), t =>
    ResponseRule {
      subject: ClassSelector { name: "dispatch-btn" },
      shape: SelfResponse,
      trigger: t,
      transition_props: "border-color",
      timing: timing,
      base_decls: [],
      response_decls: [ decl(prop: BorderColor, value: "var(--text)") ],
    })
}

fn dispatch_btn_receipt_rules(timing: TimingToken) -> List<BuildRule> {
  [
    ResponseRule {
      subject: ClassSelector { name: "dispatch-btn" },
      shape: SelfResponse,
      trigger: OnActive,
      transition_props: "background-color",
      timing: timing,
      base_decls: [],
      response_decls: [ decl(prop: Background, value: "var(--border)") ],
    },
  ]
}

data detent_realization_note: String = "Every transform rule is READ from the detent table, never restated beside it. That is the whole reason the table exists as data: a position written once in a table and again in a rule builder is the same fact in two places, and the copy that drifts is always the one nobody is looking at. So a position response (Lift, Depress, Settle) resolves its state in the table and emits whatever the table says; if the state is missing the realization yields NO rules, which surfaces through the existing unrealized-behaviour census as a counted row rather than as a silently absent transition.

AtRest emits a StaticRule because the resting transform is the property's base value — it must be DECLARED for a transition to have somewhere to return to, and it carries no timing because arriving at rest is governed by the edge that leaves the other state."

fn detent_rules(class_name: String, detents: List<Detent>, state: String, timing: TimingToken) -> List<BuildRule> {
  match detent_named(detents: detents, state: state) {
    Absent => []
    Present { value: d } => {
      let transform = decl(prop: Transform, value: detent_transform_wire(d: d))
      match d.reach {
        AtRest => [ StaticRule { selector: ClassSelector { name: class_name }, decls: [transform] } ]
        ByTrigger { trigger } => [
          ResponseRule {
            subject: ClassSelector { name: class_name },
            shape: SelfResponse,
            trigger: trigger,
            transition_props: "transform",
            timing: timing,
            base_decls: [],
            response_decls: [transform],
          },
        ]
      }
    }
  }
}

fn control_lift_rules(class_name: String, detents: List<Detent>, timing: TimingToken) -> List<BuildRule> {
  concat(
    detent_rules(class_name: class_name, detents: detents, state: detent_rest, timing: timing),
    detent_rules(class_name: class_name, detents: detents, state: detent_hover, timing: timing))
}

fn control_depress_rules(class_name: String, detents: List<Detent>, timing: TimingToken) -> List<BuildRule> {
  detent_rules(class_name: class_name, detents: detents, state: detent_pressed, timing: timing)
}

fn dispatch_btn_hold_rules(timing: TimingToken) -> List<BuildRule> {
  detent_rules(
    class_name: "dispatch-btn",
    detents: roadmap_dispatch_detents,
    state: detent_requested,
    timing: timing)
}

fn dispatch_btn_settle_rules(timing: TimingToken) -> List<BuildRule> {
  detent_rules(
    class_name: "dispatch-btn",
    detents: roadmap_dispatch_detents,
    state: detent_accepted,
    timing: timing)
}

fn roadmap_realize_behavior(row: BehaviorRow) -> RealizedBehavior {
  let e = row.element
  let r = response_key(r: row.response)
  let v = verb_key(v: row.verb)
  if element_id_eq(a: e, b: DispatchBtnElement) && r == "brighten" {
    RealizedRules { rules: dispatch_btn_brighten_rules(timing: row.timing) }
  } else if element_id_eq(a: e, b: DispatchBtnElement) && r == "receipt" {
    RealizedRules { rules: dispatch_btn_receipt_rules(timing: row.timing) }
  } else if element_id_eq(a: e, b: DispatchBtnElement) && r == "lift" {
    RealizedRules {
      rules: control_lift_rules(
        class_name: "dispatch-btn",
        detents: roadmap_dispatch_detents,
        timing: row.timing)
    }
  } else if element_id_eq(a: e, b: DispatchBtnElement) && r == "depress" && v == "dispatch" {
    RealizedRules {
      rules: control_depress_rules(
        class_name: "dispatch-btn",
        detents: roadmap_dispatch_detents,
        timing: row.timing)
    }
  } else if element_id_eq(a: e, b: DispatchBtnElement) && r == "depress" && v == "await" {
    RealizedRules { rules: dispatch_btn_hold_rules(timing: row.timing) }
  } else if element_id_eq(a: e, b: DispatchBtnElement) && r == "settle" {
    RealizedRules { rules: dispatch_btn_settle_rules(timing: row.timing) }
  } else if element_id_eq(a: e, b: SoundToggleElement) && r == "brighten" {
    RealizedRules { rules: sound_toggle_brighten_rules(timing: row.timing) }
  } else if element_id_eq(a: e, b: SoundToggleElement) && r == "lift" {
    RealizedRules {
      rules: control_lift_rules(
        class_name: sound_toggle_class,
        detents: roadmap_sound_toggle_detents,
        timing: row.timing)
    }
  } else if element_id_eq(a: e, b: SoundToggleElement) && r == "depress" {
    RealizedRules {
      rules: control_depress_rules(
        class_name: sound_toggle_class,
        detents: roadmap_sound_toggle_detents,
        timing: row.timing)
    }
  } else {
    UnrealizedBehavior { element: element_id_key(id: e), verb: v, response: r }
  }
}

fn sound_toggle_brighten_rules(timing: TimingToken) -> List<BuildRule> {
  map(approach_triggers(), t =>
    ResponseRule {
      subject: ClassSelector { name: sound_toggle_class },
      shape: SelfResponse,
      trigger: t,
      transition_props: "border-color",
      timing: timing,
      base_decls: [],
      response_decls: [ decl(prop: BorderColor, value: "var(--focus)") ],
    })
}

fn roadmap_unrealized_behaviors(rows: List<BehaviorRow>) -> List<RealizedBehavior> {
  fold(rows, init: [], f: (acc, row) => match roadmap_realize_behavior(row: row) {
    RealizedRules { rules: _ } => acc
    UnrealizedBehavior { element, verb, response } =>
      concat(acc, [UnrealizedBehavior { element: element, verb: verb, response: response }])
  })
}

fn roadmap_behavior_rules(rows: List<BehaviorRow>) -> List<BuildRule> {
  fold(rows, init: [], f: (acc, row) => match roadmap_realize_behavior(row: row) {
    RealizedRules { rules } => concat(acc, rules)
    UnrealizedBehavior { element: _, verb: _, response: _ } => acc
  })
}

fn roadmap_dispatch_detent_defects() -> List<DetentEdgeDefect> {
  detent_edge_defects(detents: roadmap_dispatch_detents, edges: roadmap_detent_edges)
}
